    <h1>Arrows Puzzle: How to Play, Full Rules, and Beginner Tips</h1>

    <p>The arrows puzzle is one of the most satisfying logic puzzles on mobile — a sliding-tile placement game where every arrow must point at its target. This guide is the complete hub: what the arrows puzzle is, the full rules broken down step by step, five beginner tips, where to play, and straight answers to the questions players ask most.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Ecffri.</div>

    <h2>What Is the Arrows Puzzle?</h2>
    <p>An arrows puzzle presents you with a grid of empty cells, a set of arrow tiles, and a set of targets scattered across the board. Your job is to place every arrow so that it points at one target — and because arrows are directional and target slots are limited, this is a pure logic problem rather than a guessing game. The most famous version is <strong>Arrows – Puzzle Escape</strong> by Ecffri, a free mobile game with more than 100 million installs. It turned the basic idea into a full campaign: hundreds of hand-made levels, locked tiles, color constraints, teleporters, and themed packs that keep introducing new twists on the core rule.</p>

    <p>What makes the arrows puzzle special is its <em>movement</em> mechanic. Arrows are not placed freely — they slide. An arrow tile can only move through empty cells, the way a tile in a sliding-block puzzle moves. That one rule transforms the game from "point arrows at targets" into "find a sequence of slides that lands every arrow in a spot from which it can see its target." The spatial planning this demands is closely related to the reasoning used in classic grid puzzles like Shikaku, where you also have to hold shape, position, and constraint in your head at the same time.</p>

    <h2>How to Play Arrows Puzzle: The Rules, Step by Step</h2>
    <p>The full rule set is small enough to learn in one sitting. Here is every rule you need, in the order you will meet it.</p>

    <div class="rule-card">
      <div class="rule-num">1</div>
      <div><strong>Every arrow must point at exactly one target.</strong><p>An arrow is "solved" when its directional arrowhead lines up with a target on the same row or column, with no locked wall in between. Target slots are limited, so each solved arrow consumes one target.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-card">
      <div class="rule-num">2</div>
      <div><strong>Arrows slide through empty cells; they are never lifted.</strong><p>Drag an arrow into an adjacent empty cell to move it. It can travel along any clear path of empty cells, exactly like a tile in a sliding puzzle. You cannot pick an arrow up and place it somewhere unreachable.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-card">
      <div class="rule-num">3</div>
      <div><strong>Locked tiles never move.</strong><p>Some levels have tiles that are fixed in place. Locked arrows already point at their target and cannot be disturbed; locked obstacles block slides and line of sight. Treat them as permanent walls.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-card">
      <div class="rule-num">4</div>
      <div><strong>Colored arrows must point at same-colored targets.</strong><p>Starting in the mid-game, arrows and targets come in colors. A blue arrow can only point at a blue target. Colors split the puzzle into independent sub-puzzles that share one board.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-card">
      <div class="rule-num">5</div>
      <div><strong>Every arrow on the board must be used.</strong><p>You cannot leave an arrow stranded. The level only completes when every arrow is placed and pointing at its own target — no strays allowed.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="rule-card">
      <div class="rule-num">6</div>
      <div><strong>The goal: all arrows placed with no target left empty.</strong><p>When the final arrow slides home and every target is pointed at, the level is solved. The game tracks your moves, so efficient solvers get a cleaner finish.</p></div>
    </div>

    <h2>Rules Deep Dive: What the Board Is Really Asking</h2>
    <p>Once you know the six rules, the game becomes a reasoning exercise in three layers.</p>
    <p><strong>Layer 1 — Geometry.</strong> An arrow's position limits its legal directions: a tile in the top row can only point right, left, or down; a corner tile has two options. Counting these constraints is the fastest way to spot a forced move, because an arrow with only one legal position must go there.</p>
    <p><strong>Layer 2 — Movement.</strong> Because arrows slide, a "correct" final spot is useless if no path reaches it. The order of slides matters: a middle arrow may need to be parked temporarily so a deeper arrow can pass, then returned to its final cell. This is the layer that separates beginners from comfortable solvers.</p>
    <p><strong>Layer 3 — Ownership.</strong> Every target slot is a resource. If two arrows both have a clear line to the same target, you must deduce which arrow gets that slot and which must be re-routed. Ownership conflicts are where most mid-game deadlocks are born — and where the <a href="/arrows-puzzle-walkthrough/">full walkthrough strategies</a> shine.</p>

    <h2>5 Beginner Tips for the Arrows Puzzle</h2>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-beginner">Tip 1</span>
      <strong>Always start with forcing moves.</strong>
      <p>Scan for any arrow with only one legal landing spot — a cell it can reach and from which it can see a target. Place it first. Every forcing move you bank shrinks the board and reveals more forcing moves. This habit alone will carry you through the first two hundred levels.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-beginner">Tip 2</span>
      <strong>Solve edges and corners first.</strong>
      <p>Board edges are permanent walls, so edge-adjacent targets can only be serviced from a small set of cells. Lock in those arrows early and the messy interior becomes a much smaller, calmer puzzle.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-beginner">Tip 3</span>
      <strong>Do not touch an arrow twice.</strong>
      <p>If you slide the same tile back and forth, you are experimenting, not solving. Undo to a clean state and hunt for the forced move you missed. A well-played level feels like a chain of confident one-way trips.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-beginner">Tip 4</span>
      <strong>Respect the colors early.</strong>
      <p>When colors appear, count arrows and targets per color before moving anything. If blue has three arrows and only two blue targets, you have already read the board wrong. Balanced counts mean everything else can be solved color by color.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <span class="pill pill-beginner">Tip 5</span>
      <strong>Learn from deadlocks instead of resetting mentally.</strong>
      <p>A deadlock is data: it tells you a chain fired in the wrong order or an ownership conflict was resolved incorrectly. Retrace the last three moves and identify the decision that caused it — then replay with the fix. Players who learn from deadlocks improve twice as fast as players who just undo them.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Where to Play the Arrows Puzzle</h2>
    <p>The official <strong>Arrows – Puzzle Escape</strong> by Ecffri is available free on Google Play and the App Store, with 100M+ installs and a steady stream of new level packs. There is no official browser version of its exact levels, so if you have a preference for playing in a browser — or you want unlimited levels with no install — the best alternative is a web-based logic game with the same spatial reasoning at its core. <a href="/">GridPaw's Shikaku</a> is exactly that: a free, no-download game where you partition a grid into numbered rectangles, exercising the same planning and constraint-tracking muscles the arrows puzzle does.</p>

